In this lesson, we'll look at the addition rule, which helps you find the chance of one thing or another happening. Imagine two events called A and B. If you want the probability of A or B occurring, you might think you just add their probabilities together, but here's the catch: A and B can overlap, meaning both can happen at the same time. When you add them, you end up counting that overlap twice, so you need to subtract it once. That's why the addition rule says the probability of A or B is the probability of A plus the probability of B, minus the probability of both happening together. Now, a special case is when A and B are mutually exclusive, meaning they can't both occur. In that situation, there's no overlap to worry about, so you just add their probabilities straight up. Finally, remember the complement rule: the probability of an event not happening is one minus the probability that it does happen. Now let's see it in action.